From 5e77082dfe68db05737fb4cdbcbdf3125e7550c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shota <monyuonyu@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:01:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 274/291] Unified: the geometry of dragging a part with a
 magnet

MagnetDrag.py: where a dragged part goes for the cursor ray. Seen from
above it slides on its own level with the grabbed point under the cursor
and climbs onto the top of other parts under its footprint (never below
where it started); seen from the side it moves in the vertical plane
facing the camera. Then the magnet, within a tolerance given in mm (about
12 pixels at the part): faces facing each other snap flush (turning the
part by up to 10 degrees), round faces onto one axis (a pin drops into a
hole as far as it fits: the bottom of a blind hole, the head of a bolt,
flush with the far side of a through hole), corners onto corners, faces
line up in one plane with faces of other parts, with guide lines along
the edges that line up. A snap holds up to 1.5 tolerances.

The faces are read once per part (numpy arrays), only for parts near the
moving one (Lazy). 200 parts: under 1 ms per move headless.
Tests: FreeCADCmd -t TestMagnetDrag (21).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
